 | `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_A_HOIST` | **ON by device probe when unset on AMD, OFF on every other vendor** — the only vendor this was measured on. Specialization constant 3 of `mul_mat_vecq.comp`; `1` = decode each row's weight block once per (row, k-slice) into registers and loop the columns doing only the B load and the integer dot, `0` = the inherited nest, which re-runs the whole A decode once per column. Only an exact `0` or `1` is taken; anything else falls back to the probe. Added 2026-09-14 (lane-235/R48b). | Scope: every `q8_1` integer-dot `mul_mat_vec` pipeline. Both nests call the same `mmvq_load_a()` / `mmvq_dot_a()` pair in the same order, so results are **bit-identical by construction** and this is a scheduling change only. `NUM_COLS == 1` folds to the inherited nest at pipeline-compile time. Probed once at device creation, because routing reads the same answer. Two narrowings ride on top: `IQ1_S`/`IQ1_M` are OFF by default on register grounds (see the next row) and the legacy-block family (`Q4_0`, `Q4_1`, `Q5_0`, `Q5_1`, `Q8_0`, `MXFP4`) is OFF at `NUM_COLS >= 6`. **`=0` is the widest switch in this group: it removes the hoisted nest AND the R48b routing that depends on it.** | Default. Use `=0` for the OFF arm of a pairing — one binary serves both arms — or on a vendor this has not been measured on. | **Correctness CLEARED, default-route performance UNMEASURED.** `test-backend-ops MUL_MAT -b Vulkan0` is 0 FAIL in both arms, and two planted reds proved the decode site live (RED-1, fires in both arms) and the hoisted nest the one serving (RED-2, red ON / green OFF). HQ forced-MMVQ pairing (`32-paired-r48bforce.txt`, 3 quiet interleaved rounds, same binary): the hoist removes a **10-15x tail cliff** at `NUM_COLS` 6..7 for `q4_K` (248320x5120 `5.1 / 3.3` → `52 / 48` GB/s; 5120x17408 `3.7 / 2.5` → `26.8 / 24.3`) and holds S-X8 flat at the bus across `n=1..8`. It **REGRESSES Q8_0 at `n>=6`** (`-25 / -17 / -24%` at `n=6/7/8` on 4096x14336, `-17%` at `n=8` on 5120x17408, `-10%` at `n=6` on 248320x5120), which is what the legacy-family `NUM_COLS >= 6` gate is for. `q5_K` at `n=1`, its only MMVQ width, is a wash. Every number here is from a `GGML_VK_FORCE_MMVQ=1` arm; a **default-routing re-pair is owed**. |
 | `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_A_HOIST_IQ1` | **OFF when unset.** Only an exact `1` turns the A-side hoist on for the `IQ1_S` and `IQ1_M` `q8_1` pipelines, and never above the global switch — `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_A_HOIST=0` forces it off regardless. Added 2026-09-14 (lane-235/R48b). | Scope: the `IQ1_S` and `IQ1_M` `q8_1` `mul_mat_vec` pipelines only. They carry the largest A state of any MMVQ type (10 and 12 registers per row against 5-6 for the K-quants) and run the most rows per workgroup (`rm_iq_int` = 4 or 8), the highest product in the register table. Gated in the HOST, not by an `#ifdef`: the shader keeps both paths, so no rebuild moves it. | Only to pair the IQ1 types specifically. | **UNMEASURED on these two types — that is exactly why they ship OFF.** The default is a register-pressure judgement (report section 3 of `OPUS-R48B-MMVQ-A-HOIST-REPORT.md`), not a measurement. Arithmetic is identical either way; correctness is covered by the same `MUL_MAT` sweep. |
 | `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_ROUTE` | **unset = R48b routing.** Only the exact string `legacy` pins `ggml_vk_should_use_mmvq()` back to the pre-R48b rules while KEEPING the hoisted nest. Added 2026-09-14 (lane-235/R48b phase 2). | Scope: two AMD routing rules, and nothing else. (i) `GGML_TYPE_SX8` → MMVQ at every width (`legacy`: opt-in via `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_MMVQ=1`). (ii) `GGML_TYPE_Q4_K` at `n >= 5` → MMVQ when `k <= 8192`, f32 above (`legacy`: f32 at every `n >= 5`). `Q5_K`, `Q8_0` and the legacy-block family are untouched in both modes. Both rules are additionally gated on the hoist being live, so on a non-AMD device or under `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_A_HOIST=0` they are already the legacy rules. **`legacy` is NOT the same arm as `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_A_HOIST=0`**: this one keeps the hoisted nest and changes only where traffic is sent, the other removes the nest as well. A pairing log must say which was used. | Use `legacy` to pair the ROUTING change alone, holding the kernel constant. | **Routing derived from the HQ forced-MMVQ pairing, not yet re-measured at default routing.** `q4_K` MMVQ+hoist vs the f32 default at `n=5..8`: 248320x5120 (k=5120) `55.7/52.1/48.0/45.6` vs `46.0/36.1/35.0/29.0` GB/s (**+21/+44/+37/+57%**), 17408x5120 (k=5120) `35.7/34.8/33.5/31.3` vs `33.2/27.9/29.9/24.4` (win), 5120x17408 (**k=17408**) `32.4/26.8/24.3/22.7` vs `37.0/30.8/31.6/27.2` (**LOSS**) — hence the `k` split. `k = 8192` is the midpoint of the two measured `k` values and is an **untested boundary**: nothing was run between 5120 and 17408. S-X8 evidence is in the `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_MMVQ` row above. |
-| `GGML_ARIFI_Q5K_MMVQ` | **`legacy` when unset — the shipped route, no default moves (F-08).** The exact string `route` admits `GGML_TYPE_Q5_K` to the A-hoisted `q8_1` MMVQ mat-vec at **`n=5..8` and `k <= 8192`**; every other `n` and every larger `k` still take the f32 dequant shader, exactly as today. The string `v2` is accepted as a NAME only and behaves as `legacy`: it is reserved for a shader change this lane did not need, and the device-init line says so out loud rather than letting a caller think it routed. Added 2026-09-17 (lane-252/R64). | Scope: the `Q5_K` arm of `ggml_vk_should_use_mmvq()` on AMD at `n > 1`, and nothing else. Additionally gated on `mmvq_a_hoist` being live and on `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_ROUTE != legacy`, so on a non-AMD device or with the hoist off it is already the legacy rule. **NOT bit-identical to `legacy` by construction** — the MMVQ path quantises B to `q8_1` and does an integer dot, where the f32 route does float madds; this is a different arithmetic, not a scheduling change, and no pairing may describe it as one. Device-init receipt on stderr: `ggml_vulkan: q5_k mmvq route: route (n=5..8, k<=8192)` / `legacy`. | **Leave it at the default until HQ has paired it served.** Set `route` to A/B the arms on one binary. The `n>=5` and `k<=8192` boundaries are **gfx1103 measurements, not portable constants** — both are re-derivable and both must be re-paired on another device class. | **MEASURED op-level, 6 interleaved rounds, one quiet hold, paired per round; served t/s UNMEASURED by the maker.** 17408x5120 (`r64-evidence/13-paired-ffn.txt`, `13-paired-ffn2.txt`), `default/route` median [min..max] wins/6: `n=5` **1.067** [1.032..1.104] 6/6, `n=6` **1.109** 6/6, `n=7` **1.095** 6/6, `n=8` **1.204** [1.185..1.266] 6/6; `n=1` 0.999 (untouched). `marg(4..8)` falls **113.8 → 5.4 us/col** against q4_K's 27 — the lane's ship criterion. Re-taken on the shipped `n>=5` binary: `n=5..8` = 1.056/1.120/1.075/1.152, 5-6/6. **The `k` gate is measured, not copied**: at 5120x17408 (`k=17408`) forced MMVQ **LOSES**, unanimously at `n=5` (0.962 [0.921..0.997], **0/6** rounds won) and near-unanimously at `n=6` (0.968, 1/6); at `n=7`/`n=8` the medians are 0.988/0.976 but the rounds split 3/6 and 2/6 and the ranges straddle 1.0, so those two are **not** established against the null band and are not claimed (`13-paired-down-head.txt`). The gate ships on the firm end, and its error direction is to NARROW the admit. On the served Q4_K_XL file **90.5% of Q5_K bytes sit at `k<=8192`** (`11-typemix.txt`), so the gate keeps nearly all of the type. **The `n<5` end is NOT established as a loss**: the shipped binary makes `route` at `n=1..4` the identical code path to `default`, and it still read 0.942 at `n=4` — a three-arm null control (`14-null-table.txt`, all legacy, same slots) bounds the slot artifact at only 1-3%, so `n=4` on this shape is **noise-dominated and the lane's least-resolved cell**. `n>=5` is the conservative bound, not a fitted one: it is where `rm_int_n()` gives the MMVQ pipeline 4 rows per workgroup instead of 1. Correctness `test -o MUL_MAT -p type_a=q5_K/q6_K` **160 cases, 0 FAIL, all three arms** (`20-correct-*`), full `MUL_MAT` sweep **0 FAIL both arms** (`21-mulmat-full-*`), planted RED in `mul_mat_vecq.comp` under `DATA_A_Q5_K && NUM_COLS>1` reddens **35 cases with `route`, 0 with `legacy`**, restored byte-identical (`40-restore-proof.txt`). 27B text: **6/6 token-identical**, 3 greedy prompts x 2 Q4_K_XL files — but that is an `n=1` decode, where this rule is **inert by construction**, so it is a no-regression receipt and NOT evidence about the routed arithmetic. **Still owed before any default moves: a PPL cell (the R48b ship rule) and a served pairing.** |
+| `GGML_ARIFI_Q5K_MMVQ` | **`legacy` when unset — the shipped route, no default moves (F-08).** The exact string `route` admits `GGML_TYPE_Q5_K` to the A-hoisted `q8_1` MMVQ mat-vec at **`n=5..8` and `k <= 8192`**; every other `n` and every larger `k` still take the f32 dequant shader, exactly as today. The string `v2` is accepted as a NAME only and behaves as `legacy`: it is reserved for a shader change this lane did not need, and the device-init line says so out loud rather than letting a caller think it routed. Added 2026-09-17 (lane-252/R64). | Scope: the `Q5_K` arm of `ggml_vk_should_use_mmvq()` on AMD at `n > 1`, and nothing else. Additionally gated on `mmvq_a_hoist` being live and on `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_ROUTE != legacy`, so on a non-AMD device or with the hoist off it is already the legacy rule. **NOT bit-identical to `legacy` by construction** — the MMVQ path quantises B to `q8_1` and does an integer dot, where the f32 route does float madds; this is a different arithmetic, not a scheduling change, and no pairing may describe it as one. Device-init receipt on stderr: `ggml_vulkan: q5_k mmvq route: route (n=5..8, k<=8192)` / `legacy`. | **Leave it at the default until HQ has paired it served.** Set `route` to A/B the arms on one binary. The `n>=5` and `k<=8192` boundaries are **gfx1103 measurements, not portable constants** — both are re-derivable and both must be re-paired on another device class. | **MEASURED op-level, 6 interleaved rounds, one quiet hold, paired per round; served t/s UNMEASURED by the maker.** 17408x5120 (`r64-evidence/13-paired-ffn.txt`, `13-paired-ffn2.txt`), `default/route` median [min..max] wins/6: `n=5` **1.067** [1.032..1.104] 6/6, `n=6` **1.109** 6/6, `n=7` **1.095** 6/6, `n=8` **1.204** [1.185..1.266] 6/6; `n=1` 0.999 (untouched). `marg(4..8)` falls **113.8 → 5.4 us/col** against q4_K's 27 — the lane's ship criterion. Re-taken on the shipped `n>=5` binary: `n=5..8` = 1.056/1.120/1.075/1.152, 5-6/6. **The `k` gate is measured, not copied**: at 5120x17408 (`k=17408`) forced MMVQ **LOSES**, unanimously at `n=5` (0.962 [0.921..0.997], **0/6** rounds won) and near-unanimously at `n=6` (0.968, 1/6); at `n=7`/`n=8` the medians are 0.988/0.976 but the rounds split 3/6 and 2/6 and the ranges straddle 1.0, so those two are **not** established against the null band and are not claimed (`13-paired-down-head.txt`). The gate ships on the firm end, and its error direction is to NARROW the admit. On the served Q4_K_XL file **90.5% of Q5_K bytes sit at `k<=8192`** (`11-typemix.txt`), so the gate keeps nearly all of the type. **The `n<5` end is NOT established as a loss**: the shipped binary makes `route` at `n=1..4` the identical code path to `default`, and it still read 0.942 at `n=4` — a three-arm null control (`14-null-table.txt`, all legacy, same slots) bounds the slot artifact at only 1-3%, so `n=4` on this shape is **noise-dominated and the lane's least-resolved cell**. `n>=5` is the conservative bound, not a fitted one: it is where `rm_int_n()` gives the MMVQ pipeline 4 rows per workgroup instead of 1. Correctness `test -o MUL_MAT -p type_a=q5_K/q6_K` **152 cases (q5_K 85/85 + q6_K 67/67), 0 FAIL, all three arms** (`20-correct-*`), full `MUL_MAT` sweep **2130/2130, 0 FAIL both arms** (`21-mulmat-full-*`), planted RED in `mul_mat_vecq.comp` under `DATA_A_Q5_K && NUM_COLS>1` gives **52/85 = 33 FAIL cases with `route`, every one at `n=5..8`, and 85/85 with `legacy`**, restored byte-identical (`40-restore-proof.txt`). 27B text: **6/6 token-identical**, 3 greedy prompts x 2 Q4_K_XL files — but that is an `n=1` decode, where this rule is **inert by construction**, so it is a no-regression receipt and NOT evidence about the routed arithmetic. **The ship rule is now discharged, by HQ and not by the maker (chain149):** PPL **at batch 8, where the route is actually exercised** (a default-batch cell runs at n=512 through `mul_mm` and would have been inert) reads **5.9918 legacy vs 5.9932 route against +/-0.143** - identical inside one stderr; served Q4_K_XL DFlash2 **tie**, plain arm **noise**, rolled40 **37/37**. Receipts under `2026-09-14-lane-234-r48-kernels/100-r64-q5k-route/` and `2026-09-02-lane-209/r64-lane-252/c24-q4kxl-27b/SEAT/AB.json`. **Expected served effect is small by construction and a large one would be a red flag:** ~**+0.7% at n=5**, ceiling ~**+1.8% at n=8**, **0% at n=4** (inert) - so a tie IS the predicted result, and a gain above ~2% means the attribution is wrong, not the lever better. **Moving the default remains an independent checker's call.** |
 | `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_TRACE` | **OFF when unset.** `1` makes `ggml_vk_should_use_mmvq()` print one deduplicated stderr line per distinct `(type, m, n, k)` decision. Added 2026-09-17 (lane-252/R64). | Scope: a wrapper around the predicate; the value returned is exactly the rule set's, so there is **no behavioural effect in either arm**. It reports the DECISION, not the dispatched pipeline — the caller clears `quantize_y` if the `q8_1` pipeline is null (`ggml-vulkan.cpp`, the `if (dmmv == nullptr)` branch). For `Q5_K`/`Q6_K` at `n<=8` that cannot fire, because those pipelines are registered unconditionally for every `NUM_COLS < mul_mat_vec_max_cols = 8`. | Diagnostic. Use it to prove which route a width takes on a given device instead of reading the rule set. | Receipts `r64-evidence/10-trace-{legacy,route,force}.txt`: on gfx1103, `legacy` gives `q5_K n=1 -> MMVQ(q8_1)` and `n=2..8 -> f32-dequant`, `q6_K -> f32-dequant` at every `n`. |
 | `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_PCA` / `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_PCA_FILE` | **ON when a PCA companion GGUF is present, and there is nothing to turn on when there is not.** `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_PCA=0` makes the loader skip the companion entirely; `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_PCA_FILE=<path>` names one instead of the auto-discovered `<model>.sx8pca.gguf` beside the model. Added 2026-09-15 (lane-242/R53). | Scope: `GGML_TYPE_SX8` weights reached through `build_lora_mm`. S-X8 v4.3 stores two signed-4-bit PCA coefficients in byte 29 of every 30-byte block, but the PCA bases and scales live only in the `.sx8v43` container - the author's GGUF converter drops them and his llama.cpp patch ignores the coefficients. `tools/sx8/extract_sx8_pca.py` pulls the bases/scales out of the container over HTTP ranges and `tools/sx8/add_sx8_pca_to_gguf.py` writes them, with the unpacked coefficient planes, into a companion GGUF (366 MB for the 4B, against a 3.9 GB rewrite of the model file). The correction is built from stock ggml ops in `src/llama-graph.cpp` (`Z0[kb] = s0*sum_t X*b0`, `Y[n] += c0*Z0 + c1*Z1`), so CPU, Vulkan and any future backend get it from one place and no kernel or shader knows about PCA. **With no companion file the map is empty and the graph is the pre-R53 graph, bit for bit.** One receipt line at load names how many tensors were attached (that line is **in no captured receipt**; the "98 tensors" figure is a code prediction, not an observation). The companion carries an **identity stamp** (`sx8pca.source.name`/`.tensor`/`.head`) that the loader compares against the model's own bytes and refuses on mismatch, so a companion from a different quantization of the same-shaped model cannot attach silently -- **the guard is UNBUILT and UNRUN as of this row, and a companion written before it warns and loads**. | Leave it alone when a companion is beside the model. Use `=0` for the OFF arm of a quality pairing on the same binary and the same files. | **QUALITY: NO MEASURABLE PPL EFFECT; speed UNMEASURED.** `test-backend-ops MUL_MAT` is 2128/2128, 0 FAIL on **Vulkan0** in both arms -- the harness printed `Skipping CPU backend`, so the CPU `MUL_MAT` surface was never swept (its synthetic tensors carry no companion, so the arms are the same code either way). `test-sx8-pca` runs a real 64-row slice of `blk.0.ffn_down.weight` against float64 goldens taken straight from the spec: the PCA term lands within `7.3e-07` of the output on CPU and `4.3e-08` on Vulkan, and the two backends agree to `8.2e-07`. A planted coefficient-sign flip moves the term by `1.5x` and is caught. The pre-existing `2.159e-03` core gap against an exact decode is the Q8_1 activation quantization of the S-X8 mat-mul, identical on both backends and untouched by R53. PPL wikitext-2 on the 4B (`llama-perplexity -c 512`, 580 chunks, one cell at a time): S-X8 PCA OFF **9.9989** (+/-0.07217), PCA ON **9.9955** (+/-0.07213), Q8_0 **9.9742**. The ON/OFF delta is **-0.034%: sign only, indistinguishable from zero** -- 0.0034 PPL is ~5% of one cell's stderr, and two shorter receipts (4-chunk smoke, 8-chunk float-route probe) have the opposite sign. It must NOT be quoted as a measured gain; the gate for a signed claim is a paired per-chunk NLL dump. The id57 GGUF **is** the same quantization run as the container the bases came from (all six block fields byte-identical over 20.6M blocks on two tensors, `r53-evidence/40-verify-gguf-matches-container.txt`, EXIT=0), so this is a measurement of the real correction, not an artefact. The correction is oriented correctly -- it moves the weights TOWARD a Q8_0 reference on every tensor tested (-3.0% to -3.4% RMS error), while a sign flip and a c0/c1 swap both move them away -- but it does **NOT** reproduce the -2.64% of the author's Table 4. On this file the dropped PCA coefficients are worth nothing measurable in perplexity at 580 chunks. Absolute values are not comparable to his: he scores 512-token windows at stride 128, `llama-perplexity` does not. **Not applied to the embedding LOOKUP** (`token_embd` via `get_rows`), which is not a matmul - that arm of the correction is OWED. |
 | `GGML_VK_SX8_MMV_ROWS` / `GGML_VK_SX8_MMV_WG` | **unset = the probed default** (workgroup = the device subgroup width when it is a multiple of 32, else 32; rows = 2). Read once at pipeline creation. Added 2026-09-11 (lane-232/R47a), extended 2026-09-11 (lane-230/R46b commit D). | Scope: every S-X8 `mul_mat_vec` pipeline, f32/f16/q8_1/id alike. An override is taken WHOLE or refused WHOLE against this device's real limits: workgroup a positive multiple of 32, within `maxComputeWorkGroupInvocations`, and `NUM_COLS * rows * wg * 4` within `maxComputeSharedMemorySize` at the WIDEST `NUM_COLS`. **Setting either variable also switches the R46b shape-tuned lookup OFF for the whole run** — a run that kept the measured table on the cells the arm does not name would be a mixed arm. A stderr receipt names APPLIED or REFUSED once per distinct pair. | Sweep instrument. Use it to pair rows 1/2/4/8 against workgroups 64/128/256 out of one binary, or to pin the pre-R46b behaviour for an A/B. Not a serve setting. | On the 780M (subgroup 64, 32 KiB shared) `rows=8/wg=256` is REFUSED by shared memory and the run keeps the default; `rows=4/wg=256` and `rows=8/wg=128` sit exactly on the 32,768-byte limit, are admitted, and measured 12/12 GREEN on `MUL_MAT` (`research/local-inference/lane-evidence/2026-09-11-lane-232-r47a-alloc-instrument/33-boundary-rows4-wg256.txt`, `34-boundary-rows8-wg128.txt`). |
